# Preventing Theme Flash in Next.js

## The Problem

When implementing dark/light mode with client-side state (useState + useEffect + localStorage), users experience a "flash" on page load:

1. Server renders with default theme (usually light)
2. Page displays with light theme
3. useEffect runs, reads localStorage, switches to dark theme
4. User sees a flash from light to dark

This is a form of layout shift and causes poor user experience.

## Solution 1: Inline Script in Layout (Blocking)

Add a small blocking script in the `<head>` that sets the theme before any content renders:

```tsx
// layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en" suppressHydrationWarning>
      <head>
        <script
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{
            __html: `
              (function() {
                var theme = localStorage.getItem('theme') || 'light';
                document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', theme);
              })();
            `,
          }}
        />
      </head>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

**Note**: `suppressHydrationWarning` on `<html>` prevents React hydration mismatch warnings since the script modifies the DOM before hydration.

## Solution 2: CSS-Only Default with `prefers-color-scheme`

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --card-bg: #262626;
    --border-color: #404040;
  }
}
```

This respects the user's OS preference without JavaScript.

## Solution 3: Cookie-Based Theme (SSR-Compatible)

Store theme in a cookie so the server can read it during SSR:

```tsx
// middleware.ts
export function middleware(request) {
  const theme = request.cookies.get('theme')?.value || 'light';
  // Pass theme to server components via headers
}
```

## Best Practice for Client Components

When the ThemeProvider must remain a client component, use the inline script approach:

```tsx
// ThemeProvider.tsx
'use client';

export function ThemeProvider({ children }) {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState<Theme>('light');
  const [mounted, setMounted] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem('theme') as Theme;
    if (savedTheme) setTheme(savedTheme);
    setMounted(true);
  }, []);

  // Don't render theme-dependent visuals until mounted
  // But the inline script in layout.tsx handles the initial flash

  return <div data-theme={theme}>{children}</div>;
}
```

The combination of the inline script (for immediate visual correctness) and the ThemeProvider (for React-managed interactivity) provides the best experience.

